\details{
\subsection{Field size}{

A one-stage size is fielded as \code{ceiling(n)}. Separate domain quotas are
fielded one by one, so their total is the sum of the whole quotas. Under
natural incidence the whole total is fielded and each domain is expected
at that total times its share, which need not be whole. A cluster design
is fielded as \verb{$operational}, and a cluster design with domains as each
row of \verb{$operational$domains}.
}

\subsection{Precision at the field size}{

The precision is that of the matching \verb{prec_*()} function evaluated at
the field size, so it can be slightly better than the target the
continuous size meets exactly. Each indicator's target and achieved
precision are reported on the scale the target was stated in (\code{moe},
\code{rmoe} or \code{cv}). A row with only a respondent floor has no precision and
reads \code{NA}. An interval method other than Wald adds its limits, at the
confidence level the size was planned for (\code{1 - alpha}).
}

\subsection{Derived sizes}{

Expected respondents are the field size times the response and
eligibility rates, and eligible respondents multiply that by
\code{eligible_per_unit}. The effective size divides the units analyzed by the
design effect. For a cluster design the design effect and the effective
size are the planning quantities of \code{\link[=design_effect]{design_effect()}} and
\code{\link[=effective_n]{effective_n()}}, which leave out finite population corrections. A
multi-indicator cluster design has no single design effect and the
summary reports none.
}

\subsection{Binding indicator}{

The binding indicator of \code{n_multi()} is the row whose requirement sets
the size, and \code{binding_cause} says what set it: \code{"precision"},
\code{"min_cases"}, \code{"min_respondents"} or \code{"min_n_domain"}. For a
multi-indicator cluster design it is the row with the largest ratio of
achieved to target cv at the field design, which need not be the row with
the worst cv. With domains it is that row in the domain with the worst cv
at the field design.
}

\subsection{Domains}{

A domain is labeled by its value when one column defines domains, and by
\code{column=value} pairs joined by commas when several do. A value holding a
comma, an equals sign, a quote or a backslash is quoted, so two domains
never share a label. \code{domain_values} holds the domain columns, one row
per row of \code{domains}.
}

\subsection{Assumptions of a multi-indicator cluster design}{

The \code{indicators} table carries each row's design quantities:
\code{unit_relvar}, \code{icc_1} through \verb{icc_\{S-1\}}, \code{var_ratio_1} through
\verb{var_ratio_\{S-1\}}, and the response rates, with \code{alpha} when a row
states a \code{moe} or \code{rmoe}. A quantity equal on every row is also
listed in \code{assumptions}, and the print shows the ones that vary in a
table of their own.

Results of \code{\link[=n_alloc]{n_alloc()}} have their own summary, described in
\link{summary.svyplan_alloc}.
}
}
